-- §3d The incidence and the diagonal, as the two things whose product has a null space.
# One row per resolved part: which fusion it goes into, and the factor that carries it there.
#
# Why the factor rides along when the rank does not depend on it
#   `Phi` is a positive diagonal, and scaling a column by a positive number cannot change which
#   columns are independent, so `rank(F Phi) = rank(F)` whatever the factors are. That is a claim
#   worth running rather than repeating: the program takes the rank twice, once with the filed
#   factors and once with every entry at one, and the two must agree.
#   Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry `units_scale_rows`.
#
# A factor that is not a number is still an edge
#   Where `asrt:Part/factor` is a typed absence or a derivation there is no figure, and the column
#   is NULL. The part is still in the fusion, so it is still a column of `F`, and the program
#   reads a NULL as one for the purpose of taking a rank. That substitution is safe for exactly
#   the reason above and for no other, so it may never be carried into a figure: what an absent
#   factor converts to is not one, it is unknown, and `composition/converted.sqlc` is where that
#   distinction is kept.
#
# Resolved parts, because an unresolved one is no column
#   A part naming a filing this corpus does not hold has no figures to convert, so it is not a
#   column of the matrix. `rank/composition_kernel.sqlc` folds the same set, which is what lets
#   the program compare a rank against a count.
-- asrt:Fusion/asrt:Part resolved, projected to the incidence and its diagonal.
SELECT p.composition                             AS "composition!",
       p.composed_layer                          AS "composed_layer!",
       p.part_filing || '/' || p.part_layer      AS "part!",
       CASE WHEN p.factor_state = 'omitted' THEN 1::double precision
            ELSE p.factor_mode::double precision END AS factor
FROM (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
ORDER BY p.composition, p.composed_layer, "part!"
